#: ../../source/argparse_README.md:129
msgid "Options:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/argparse_README.md:131
msgid ""
"```action``` - The basic type of action to be taken when this argument is"
" encountered at the command line."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/argparse_README.md:132
msgid "```nargs```- The number of command-line arguments that should be consumed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/argparse_README.md:133
msgid ""
"```constant``` - A constant value required by some action and nargs "
"selections."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/argparse_README.md:134
msgid ""
"```defaultValue``` - The value produced if the argument is absent from "
"the command line."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/argparse_README.md:135
msgid ""
"```type``` - The type to which the command-line argument should be "
"converted."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/argparse_README.md:136
msgid "```choices``` - A container of the allowable values for the argument."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/argparse_README.md:137
msgid ""
"```required``` - Whether or not the command-line option may be omitted "
"(optionals only)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/argparse_README.md:138
msgid "```help``` - A brief description of what the argument does."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/argparse_README.md:139
msgid "```metavar``` - A name for the argument in usage messages."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/argparse_README.md:140
msgid ""
"```dest``` - The name of the attribute to be added to the object returned"
" by parseArgs()."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/argparse_README.md:142
msgid ""
"Details in [original add_argument "
"guide](http://docs.python.org/dev/library/argparse.html#the-add-argument-"
"method)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/argparse_README.md:145
msgid "Action (some details)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/argparse_README.md:148
msgid ""
"ArgumentParser objects associate command-line arguments with actions. "
"These actions can do just about anything with the command-line arguments "
"associated with them, though most actions simply add an attribute to the "
"object returned by parseArgs(). The action keyword argument specifies how"
" the command-line arguments should be handled. The supported actions are:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/argparse_README.md:154
msgid ""
"```store``` - Just stores the argument’s value. This is the default "
"action."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/argparse_README.md:155
msgid ""
"```storeConst``` - Stores value, specified by the const keyword argument."
" (Note that the const keyword argument defaults to the rather unhelpful "
"None.) The 'storeConst' action is most commonly used with optional "
"arguments, that specify some sort of flag."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/argparse_README.md:159
msgid ""
"```storeTrue``` and ```storeFalse``` - Stores values True and False "
"respectively. These are special cases of 'storeConst'."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/argparse_README.md:161
msgid ""
"```append``` - Stores a list, and appends each argument value to the "
"list. This is useful to allow an option to be specified multiple times."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/argparse_README.md:163
msgid ""
"```appendConst``` - Stores a list, and appends value, specified by the "
"const keyword argument to the list. (Note, that the const keyword "
"argument defaults is None.) The 'appendConst' action is typically used "
"when multiple arguments need to store constants to the same list."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/argparse_README.md:167
msgid ""
"```count``` - Counts the number of times a keyword argument occurs. For "
"example, used for increasing verbosity levels."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/argparse_README.md:169
msgid ""
"```help``` - Prints a complete help message for all the options in the "
"current parser and then exits. By default a help action is automatically "
"added to the parser. See ArgumentParser for details of how the output is "
"created."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/argparse_README.md:172
msgid ""
"```version``` - Prints version information and exit. Expects a `version=`"
" keyword argument in the addArgument() call."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/argparse_README.md:175
msgid ""
"Details in [original action "
"guide](http://docs.python.org/dev/library/argparse.html#action)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/argparse_README.md:178
msgid "Sub-commands"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/argparse_README.md:181
msgid "ArgumentParser.addSubparsers()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/argparse_README.md:183
msgid ""
"Many programs split their functionality into a number of sub-commands, "
"for example, the svn program can invoke sub-commands like `svn checkout`,"
" `svn update`, and `svn commit`. Splitting up functionality this way can "
"be a particularly good idea when a program performs several different "
"functions which require different kinds of command-line arguments. "
"`ArgumentParser` supports creation of such sub-commands with "
"`addSubparsers()` method. The `addSubparsers()` method is normally called"
" with no arguments and returns an special action object. This object has "
"a single method `addParser()`, which takes a command name and any "
"`ArgumentParser` constructor arguments, and returns an `ArgumentParser` "
"object that can be modified as usual."
msgstr ""

